<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Caranqui</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1495 CE (approx.) · capital Caranqui</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A confederation of chiefdoms in the northern highlands of Ecuador that held out against the Incas for years before a bloody defeat at Yahuarcocha.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Caranqui, or Karanki, lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imbabura-province">Imbabura</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pichincha-province">Pichincha</a> highlands of northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Ecuador</a>. From about 800 they built large earthen mounds called tolas, including platform mounds with ramps at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cochasqui">Cochasquí</a>, and formed an alliance with neighbouring peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayambi">Cayambi</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Caranqui were ruled by hereditary lords who controlled farming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a> on the volcanic slopes and trade with lowland peoples. Their tola mounds served as residences for chiefs, ceremonial platforms and burials. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cochasqui">Cochasquí</a>, with fifteen pyramids, was a major centre. Local leaders were bound together by marriage and exchange rather than a single state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> rulers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Túpac Inca Yupanqui</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a> fought long campaigns in the north. Around 1495 Huayna Capac crushed the Caranqui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayambi">Cayambi</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahuarcocha">Lake Yahuarcocha</a>, the 'lake of blood', named after the massacre. The region became an Inca province before the Spanish conquest.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 800 CE – 1495 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South America</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:75.62%;width:13.83%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1495 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Caranqui</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Local cults</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cara</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ecuador</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Karanki, Cara</dd></dl></aside></div>

